Two third-party libraries whose own source already knows this platform is not
one of the ones they support, and whose BUILD.gn argues with them.

cpuinfo is the sharper of the two. src/init.c ends its dispatch with

    #else
      cpuinfo_log_error("operating system is not supported in cpuinfo");
    #endif

and cpuinfo_initialize() then returns false - which is cpuinfo's designed
answer for an operating system it has no back end for, present since long
before this port existed. Its BUILD.gn compiles the Linux back end anyway,
because GN asks is_linux; those sources need /sys/devices/system/cpu and
glibc's CPU_SETSIZE, and struct cpuinfo_processor's linux_id member is behind
#if defined(__linux__) in cpuinfo's own header - so the build compiles code
that reads a field the preprocessor has already removed. Gating the back end
on has_linux_kernel makes the two agree. Nothing is stubbed and no source is
written: what is left is the path cpuinfo takes on any system it does not
know.

cpuinfo is in this build because Blink's //components/language_detection
uses TensorFlow Lite, which uses XNNPACK and ruy, which ask cpuinfo what they
are running on.

Dawn's SystemUtils.cpp is the second, and it is M159's ANGLE sentence once
more at one function's scale. It has two of these chains -

    #else
    #error "Implement GetExecutablePath for your platform."
    #else
    #error "Implement GetModulePath for your platform."

- and a third above them choosing which headers to include. So patch 0043's
generic POSIX arm is not enough on its own: a generic arm has to EXIST in
each chain, and the include one has to be there or the other two compile
without unistd.h.

GetExecutablePath reads /proc/self/exe, which is what the Linux arm does -
that interface is Linux's and other systems have copied it - and where there
is no procfs the readlink fails and it returns {}, which is what the Fuchsia
arm returns on purpose. GetModulePath is dladdr(3) and realpath(3), which are
POSIX rather than any one system's, so the POSIX arm joins the three that
already share that body.

